From 8cbe51b71438ecf6457556313d5f5fa149f23bf9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Manrique <125496885+hashd1ve@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 13:20:12 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] feat(lanes): a prompt pass runs in pieces between the
 other streams' rounds (TF_FILL_CHUNKS, TF_FILL_ROUNDS)

A prompt pass ran whole inside its admission, so every stream that was decoding stopped until it ended: on two GB10s
a 400K-token prompt (a restarted agent session, or one whose state was lost) held the other sessions for ~4 min.

The backend may offer prefill_part, the same pass in pieces of at most N chunks. When it does, TF_FILL_CHUNKS is set
and another stream decodes, the lane core runs only the first piece at admission; the stream is then `filling` and
fillStep runs a piece after every TF_FILL_ROUNDS rounds of the live streams (at once when none decodes), then draws
its first token and joins the rounds. One pass fills at a time: the host admits the next request when it ends. A
cancel or an error in a piece ends that request alone.

dsv41: prefillFn becomes an opening (slot, extent, images, rank 1's fill, the kept state) and resumable chunks; the
chunk loop's state lives in Lanes.fill between pieces. A piece ends with the read-ahead joined and the prompt's ids
gone, as rank 1 does at any other frame, so both ranks run the same frames in the same order; rank 1 reads ahead
again at the next chunk. Rounds leave the fill's view, rings, spans and image rows alone; the Engram read-ahead of a
chunk is redone when none is pending. Unset, every pass runs whole as before.

Test: a lane host with a stream decoding admits a six-chunk prompt; the decoding stream delivers tokens between
every two pieces, and the new reply equals the fake's continuation of its prompt.
